  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Tricholoma robustipes is a robust, terrestrial mushroom in the Tricholomataceae family characterized by its stout stalk and grayish to brownish cap. It is found in New England woodlands, particularly in association with coniferous and mixed forests.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Stout Stalk",
      "description": "The most distinctive feature is its thick, sturdy stem (stipe) that is noticeably robust and often bulbous at the base",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap Color",
      "description": "Grayish-brown to dark brown cap with a convex shape when young, often becoming flattened with age",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "White Gills",
      "description": "Crowded white to cream-colored gills that are free to slightly attached and do not darken significantly with age",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Tricholoma robustipes grows on the forest floor in coniferous and mixed woodlands throughout New England, typically appearing in fall months.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like many Tricholoma species, T. robustipes has a mild, sometimes slightly fruity odor that can help distinguish it from toxic look-alikes in the field.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
